        "heading_3mwtjG": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "title": "<p>Types of Collagen</p>",
            "size": 120,
            "text_position": "left",
            "heading_highlight_type": "underline-hand-drawn",
            "highlight_color": "#000000",
            "highlight_text_color": "",
            "spacing_bottom": 12
          }
        },
        "text_GDcEKF": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Collagen is a structural protein that acts as the glue holding your body together. It’s found in skin, tendons, ligaments, and bones, providing strength and elasticity. Among the different types of collagen, Type I is primarily found in the skin, tendons, and bones, where it provides tensile strength. Type II is located in cartilage, offering cushioning and flexibility, while Type III is found in reticular fibers, contributing to the structure of organs.</p>",
            "size": 100,
            "text_position": "left",
            "spacing_bottom": 12
          }
        }
      },
