        "heading_Lyx9KJ": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "title": "<p>Why Do We Lose Collagen?</p>",
            "size": 120,
            "text_position": "left",
            "heading_highlight_type": "underline-hand-drawn",
            "highlight_color": "#000000",
            "highlight_text_color": "",
            "spacing_bottom": 12
          }
        },
        "text_baBpGN": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Collagen production begins to decline in our mid-20s, decreasing by about 1% per year. By our 40s, we’ve lost approximately 30% of our collagen, reaching 50% in our 50s, and up to 80% by our 60s. This decline accelerates after menopause, with women losing up to 30% of their skin’s collagen within the first five years post-menopause. This results in visible effects such as fine lines and wrinkles, a loss of skin elasticity and firmness, thinning and brittle hair, and weak, peeling nails.</p>",
            "size": 100,
            "text_position": "left",
            "spacing_bottom": 12
          }
        }
      },
